"""
Multi Galaxy Features
=====================

These features build on the topic’s main example. Each introduction explains the method and its trade-offs; the worked scripts retain their model choices and detailed calculations.

__Choose a Feature__

- [Advanced/Double Source Plane Lens](https://github.com/PyAutoLabs/autolens_workspace/blob/main/notebooks/multi_galaxy/features/advanced/double_source_plane_lens/start_here.ipynb): A double source-plane lens contains background sources at two different redshifts behind the foreground deflectors. Their light is traced through the relevant sequence of planes to predict the observed image.
- [Advanced/Mass Stellar Dark](https://github.com/PyAutoLabs/autolens_workspace/blob/main/notebooks/multi_galaxy/features/advanced/mass_stellar_dark/start_here.ipynb): A decomposed mass model separates the mass associated with stellar light from a dark-halo contribution. It provides an alternative to describing the total mass with a single profile.
- [Advanced/Operated Light Profile](https://github.com/PyAutoLabs/autolens_workspace/blob/main/notebooks/multi_galaxy/features/advanced/operated_light_profile/start_here.ipynb): An operated light profile describes emission after an instrumental operation has already been included in the profile. For imaging, the common example is a profile that already describes the PSF-blurred appearance of compact emission.
- [Advanced/Shapelets](https://github.com/PyAutoLabs/autolens_workspace/blob/main/notebooks/multi_galaxy/features/advanced/shapelets/start_here.ipynb): Shapelets describe light as a weighted combination of basis functions. Unlike a single smooth light profile, the basis contains functions with different spatial structures, allowing their sum to describe more complicated galaxy morphology.
- [Advanced/Sky Background](https://github.com/PyAutoLabs/autolens_workspace/blob/main/notebooks/multi_galaxy/features/advanced/sky_background/start_here.ipynb): The background contributes light across an image in addition to the galaxy or lens being studied. If a background contribution remains in the data, it can be included in the model rather than attributed to the target's emission.
- [Advanced/Subhalo](https://github.com/PyAutoLabs/autolens_workspace/blob/main/notebooks/multi_galaxy/features/advanced/subhalo/start_here.ipynb): A dark matter subhalo can perturb lensed emission without supplying visible light of its own. The effect depends on the halo properties, its location relative to the arcs and the larger lens model.
- [Extra Galaxies](https://github.com/PyAutoLabs/autolens_workspace/blob/main/notebooks/multi_galaxy/features/extra_galaxies/start_here.ipynb): A target rarely occupies an otherwise empty field. Nearby galaxies may contribute light inside the region being fitted, and a lensing model may also need to account for their deflection of the light rays.
- [Linear Light Profiles](https://github.com/PyAutoLabs/autolens_workspace/blob/main/notebooks/multi_galaxy/features/linear_light_profiles/start_here.ipynb): A linear light profile has the same spatial form as an ordinary light profile, but its intensity is solved during each likelihood evaluation instead of being sampled by the non-linear search.
- [Multi Gaussian Expansion](https://github.com/PyAutoLabs/autolens_workspace/blob/main/notebooks/multi_galaxy/features/multi_gaussian_expansion/start_here.ipynb): A multi Gaussian expansion (MGE) represents a galaxy's light as the sum of many Gaussian profiles. Each Gaussian describes emission on a particular spatial scale; together, they can provide a flexible model of the galaxy.
- [No Lens Light](https://github.com/PyAutoLabs/autolens_workspace/blob/main/notebooks/multi_galaxy/features/no_lens_light/start_here.ipynb): Some lensing datasets contain no visible foreground lens light. This may be a property of the observation or a consequence of subtracting a foreground-light model before the lens analysis.
- [Pixelization](https://github.com/PyAutoLabs/autolens_workspace/blob/main/notebooks/multi_galaxy/features/pixelization/start_here.ipynb): A pixelization represents light using a mesh whose brightness values are solved during the fit. It provides a flexible alternative to describing all of the emission with a small collection of analytic profiles.
- [Scaling Relation](https://github.com/PyAutoLabs/autolens_workspace/blob/main/notebooks/multi_galaxy/features/scaling_relation/start_here.ipynb): A scaling relation connects the properties of a galaxy population through a shared rule, rather than giving every member a completely independent model. In these lensing examples, member luminosities help determine their mass normalizations.

For the common setup, begin with [multi_galaxy/start_here.ipynb](https://github.com/PyAutoLabs/autolens_workspace/blob/main/notebooks/multi_galaxy/start_here.ipynb). Run Python scripts from the workspace root.
"""
